// Virgil's Firestore security rules: nothing may read, nothing may write.
//
// Deploy with the checked-in Firebase configuration, from the repository root:
//
//   npx --no-install firebase deploy --only firestore:rules --project "$PROJECT_ID"
//
// It needs the GCP project registered as a Firebase project first
// (`firebase projects:addfirebase`) and `firebaserules.googleapis.com` enabled —
// both are installation prerequisites. `deploy/build.sh` enables the rules API
// and `deploy/apply.sh` publishes this deny-all ruleset before either Cloud Run
// resource. The console remains an emergency fallback: Firestore → Rules →
// paste → Publish.
//
// This header used to say `gcloud firestore databases update --rules`. **That
// flag has never existed** — `databases update` takes `--async`,
// `--concurrency-mode`, `--database`, `--delete-protection`, `--enable-pitr`
// and `--type`, `gcloud firestore` has no `rules` group, and `gcloud firebase`
// is Test Lab only (checked 2026-08-21). Security rules are a Firebase surface;
// gcloud does not reach them. `deploy/CLOUD_RUN.md` §9 carries the full
// sequence and `deploy-config.test.ts` now fails if either document prints the
// dead command again.
//
// This is the exactly-correct rule for this product rather than a placeholder
// tightened later, and the argument is short enough to check. Security rules
// govern requests that arrive from a *client* SDK — Web, Android, iOS. **There
// is no such path in this repository.** The extension speaks HTTP to
// `runner/src/service.ts` and holds no Google credential of any kind; the only
// code in the tree that opens a Firestore client is
// `adapters/src/firestore-store.ts`, which runs inside the Cloud Run Job and the
// Cloud Run service under the runtime service account. Server credentials go
// through the Admin SDK, and **Admin credentials bypass security rules
// entirely** — so denying everything costs this deployment exactly nothing while
// closing the one door nobody built.
//
// What it prevents is the failure that has no symptom until it has a very large
// one: a database created in test mode is open to anyone with the project id
// until its thirty days expire, and Virgil's board is a record of every page a
// person chose to keep. A deployment with no rules file does not have "no
// rules"; it has whatever the console last defaulted to.
//
// The corollary, so it is not discovered as a surprise: the day a client SDK
// ever does become the right answer — a mobile capture surface, a browser
// reading its own board directly — this file is the thing that stops it, and
// that is the point. It should stop it, and the rules that replace it should be
// written per collection by whoever builds that surface.

rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
